hat we did why now why not in the in  the spring statement i mean this idea of  a windfall tax has been kicking around  for for several months now well on on  the  the levy that we're putting on energy  companies as i said we wanted to take  the time to get that right so we could  also incentivize investment that is a  very different feature of how we're  doing this it's an important one and i  think it was important to take the time  to get that right but why now that's a  perfectly fair question i've always been  consistent and i've said since the  spring earlier in the spring that i was  prepared to do more and we would do more  but it was right to wait until we had  some more certainty about what would  happen to energy bills in the autumn  they're the biggest driver of the  increase in responsibility so if i could  they're the biggest driver of the cost  of living and it wasn't until this week  we heard from offgem who are the  regulator that sets those prices that we  now have a bit of clarity about that and  that meant i could size the support  appropriately yeah okay but the office  for budget responsibility pointed out in  the spring statement that the energy cap  could be rising by october to two  thousand eight hundred percent and the  information was already there no and and  the key thing is could uh and the the  office budget responsibility are not the  independent regulator that set the  energy price cap they're one of many  people who have a view on what it might  have been and the reality is the price  cap is set over a period from february  through to the summer so it simply isn't  the case that anyone had any good idea  about what it was at the beginning of  that period now we're further through we  do have a good idea we heard from the  regulator on tuesday i think it was uh  from off gem and we've acted i think  quite quickly in making sure that we can  provide support and give people  reassurance do you understand why  several of your colleagues and quite  high profile colleagues have suggested  that this is not a particularly  conservative announcement i mean you had  uh the head of the ifs quoted in the  times today saying that gordon brown  would be pleased with this particular  announcement given how we distributed  this today well i think when we're  dealing as a country with the type of  challenge that we now face with  inflation running where it is  i think the right response from a  compassionate conservative government is  as we have done by the past two years to  stand by people at a time of need and  that's why it's particularly important  that those most vulnerable get the most  support that's what you saw yesterday  but we also recognize that with bills  going up on this scale everyone is going  to feel the pinch and that's why we  wanted to make sure that there was  support available for everyone on a more  universal basis 400 pounds this autumn  on top of the 150 pounds council tax  rebate that has already gone in place  you know i think that's the right  balance but we've also done it in a  responsible way by helping to raise  money to pay for some of the measures of  course there's not being a budget we  didn't have the kind of the detail that  you might might usually get the office  for budget responsibility hasn't been  able to go through it so some specific  questions on on costing that you  presumably have done what effect does  the treasury think that this will have  on on inflation well as you said that  will be a matter for the office for  budget responsibility you must have done  your own when they do their formal  independent uh review and analysis i  think it's  our estimate my view is that it will  have a minimal impact on inflation and  that's for two what do you mean by  minimum one percentage point no  much much less than that minimal and the  reason for that is twofold one is that  because what we're doing is  very targeted at those most in need  and and secondly because we're also  raising money to help pay for it the  combination of those two things is the  responsible approach because even though  we are as you said supporting the  economy we want to make sure that we  don't make the inflation situation worse  and you know doing what some people  might say is you should just spend even  more or borrow even more i think that  would be irresponsible because that  would risk driving up inflation interest  rates and people's mortgages and that's  not something that i think we should be  doing sure but you you say that it's  targeted at most in need i do wonder  then why you haven't means tested the  400 pounds payment i'm going to be  slightly churlish i will not be the only  person who makes this point this morning  it was just a week ago that you and your  wife appeared in the sunday times rich  list you know fast forward to yesterday  and the most eye-catching policy that  you announced yesterday to you know to  target those who are most in need ends  up with both you and i ending up 400  pounds better off i mean we don't need  the money well so in terms of you look  at the overall package that we announced  yesterday three quarters of the funding  is going to the most vulnerable  households and pensioners right but  let's talk about the question  the scale of the four hundred people so  you're going to hear what even i'm going  to receive yes and you and i and you and  i don't need it but actually lots and  lots and lots of people why are we  receiving anything because  when you're trying to help people on  that scale when you need to get help to  tens of millions of people right  actually you've got a choice how best  can you deliver that and the reality is  when you're talking about that number of  people there really aren't many ways to  do it and we wanted to make sure that  this was as universal as possible we had  another alternative of doing that which  was a council tax rebate which is where  you can try and  exclude people right at the top in very  large houses but you know what we tried  that and we tried to do it with a  discretionary fund and it's worked  reasonably well but there are lots of  cases of people who will say hang on i  happen to live in this you know  expensive looking house or in a high  council tax ban house but i need help  too so actually this being universal  means that we avoid all of those  problems and really do get help to  everyone who needs it and you like me if  i'm sure like me you can also give that  money to charity if you don't need it  and that's that's great which which is  great but you know it strikes me that  rich people didn't get rich by handing  back free money that they received from  the government it's good for you that  you're going to donate it to charity a  wager most people who receive it won't  oh well i'm done most people i'm sure  you will and set an example but i said  if i think it's incumbent on someone to  say well here's the alternative  how would you else how else would you  get the support to those on middle  incomes who are working hard who do feel  they need support and i hear a lot from  them every week just because they're not  on benefits or they don't they're not  pensioners they still actually think  this is tough for me it's a 1 200 pound  increase in my energy bill it's right  that the government's on my side and i  do want to be on the side of those  people if there's another way to help  those people whilst figuring out the the  smaller minority of very wealthy people  who don't need it i would be very open  to it but actually when you're trying to  deliver support in my job practically to  tens of millions of people you've got to  just figure out how best to do that and  i do think this is the right way one of  the the electricity generators a lot of  people were suspecting that they may  well have been included in the windfall  tax they weren't  just trying to understand why well what  we said if you look at the language i  use and i was very precise about this  it is the case that there are we think  extraordinary profits being made in that  sector as a result again of elevated gas  prices because of the war in ukraine but  what we want to do and we are going to  do urgently is understand the scale of  those profits and then decide on the  appropriate next step so they're going  to have to cough up at some point just  not yet well we do have plans in place  to reform that market so other countries  in europe like france italy spain  portugal greece have all taken steps to  reform that market to correct that now  we have a plan to do the same thing so  what we're sitting down now is figuring  out well how quickly can that be put in  place and in the interim what would be  the appropriate steps just one final  question on what we had yesterday in  terms of um pensions and benefits if  inflation gets to 10 or or even higher  what is your estimate of how much more  we will have it be having to pay in  terms of pensions and benefits ed conway  did a little bit of a kind of a back of  a [ __ ] packet calculation yesterday and  he came up with a figure we can bring it  up here of around about 25 billion  pounds worth of additional expenditure  is he in the ballpark oh well actually  you talked about the office for budget  responsibility who are our independent  forecaster in the spring statement they  have already included the impact of  pensions and benefits next year going up  by a relatively  large amount relative to inflation is  that is that bigger i mean it's not  bigger i precisely recognize but  actually the numbers that we have do  assume that that happens and that  actually should give people reassurance  that next year actually their pensions  and benefits will be rising well in  excess of what inflation is currently  forecasted to be the support we've  provided yesterday will help bridge  people from here to there and i think it  will be very significant it's meaningful  and will make an enormous difference  particularly to those most vulnerable in  our society i bet you thought you were  going to get through an interview  without talking about parties and dining  strip i'm afraid we have to um one thing  that that has been striking a lot of  people myself included  in terms of the prime minister's defense  is that he felt that it was necessary  for him to attend leaving parties for  members of his staff  why  well i think the prime minister has has  rightly  explained his actions and taken full  responsibility for everything that  happened in downing street he's  apologized profusely for it and i think  most importantly as well has as he said  just the other day learnt the lessons  from what's happened and made changes  and i see that because it's also a  building where i work and he's made  significant changes to how downing  street runs which is the right thing to  do and he has and he's actually  committed and he's more determined about  delivering for the british people  his defense has been that he felt that  it was part of the job to pitch up and  and say goodbye to people who are  leaving leaving  i just wonder what planet the prime  minister is on in the middle of a viral  pandemic no one else was doing that i  spent a year siloed away from the people  who work on this very program i saw a  security guard i saw a floor manager and  i saw the lady who cleans our studio for  about the best part of a year that was  it and i'm not running the country the  prime minister went from meeting of  cobra meeting of cabinet meeting with  advisors partying the flat a leaving do  i mean this was at a time of a viral  pandemic why was the prime minister  putting himself in harm's way in that  fashion well  these these matters have all been now  fully looked at by both sue grey  independently and also by the police  they've reached all their conclusions  the prime minister's apologized and made  a statement and importantly now made  changes in how downing street runs i do  think that's the right approach and he  has my full support in carrying on now  and delivering for the british people  richie good to have you in the studio  thanks for joining us

